=== lesson
id: algo-merge
title: Merge sort
summary: Merge sort splits the list in half, sorts each half with merge sort, and merges the two sorted halves with two pointers. You'll write the merge, then the recursive sort, see why it takes O(n log n) steps on every list, what its O(n) extra space buys, and why it's stable.

=== learn
### Merging two sorted lists
Merge sort is built on one operation: **merging** two lists that are already sorted into one sorted list. Put a pointer at the front of each list. The smallest item of all is one of the two front items, so compare them, take the smaller one, and move that list's pointer on. Here's `[2, 5, 8]` merged with `[3, 4, 9]`:

| left front | right front | take | merged so far |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 2 | 3 | 2 | [2] |
| 5 | 3 | 3 | [2, 3] |
| 5 | 4 | 4 | [2, 3, 4] |
| 5 | 9 | 5 | [2, 3, 4, 5] |
| 8 | 9 | 8 | [2, 3, 4, 5, 8] |
| (none left) | 9 | 9 | [2, 3, 4, 5, 8, 9] |

When one list runs out, the rest of the other list is already in order, and bigger than everything taken so far, so it goes on the end as it is. Each step takes one item, so merging lists with n items in total takes O(n) steps.

### The merge code
`i` and `j` are the two pointers. The loop runs while both lists still have items; then whatever is left of either list goes on the end:
```python
def merge(left, right):
    merged = []
    i = 0
    j = 0
    while i < len(left) and j < len(right):
        if left[i] <= right[j]:
            merged.append(left[i])
            i += 1
        else:
            merged.append(right[j])
            j += 1
    return merged + left[i:] + right[j:]

print(merge([2, 5, 8], [3, 4, 9]), merge([1, 2], []), merge([4], [1, 2, 3]))
```
```output
[2, 3, 4, 5, 8, 9] [1, 2] [1, 2, 3, 4]
```
When the loop ends, at least one of `left[i:]` and `right[j:]` is empty, so adding both is safe. On a tie, `left[i] <= right[j]` takes the item from the left list first; that matters later for stability. Animation 1 runs this merge.

### Divide and conquer
To sort a whole list, merge sort **divides** it: it splits the list into two halves and sorts each half the same way, with merge sort. That's recursion, and it stops at lists of 0 or 1 items, which are already sorted. Then it **conquers**: it merges the two sorted halves. Here's `[38, 27, 43, 3, 9, 82, 10, 5]`:

| step | lists |
| --- | --- |
| split | [38, 27, 43, 3] [9, 82, 10, 5] |
| split | [38, 27] [43, 3] [9, 82] [10, 5] |
| split | [38] [27] [43] [3] [9] [82] [10] [5] |
| merge pairs | [27, 38] [3, 43] [9, 82] [5, 10] |
| merge pairs | [3, 27, 38, 43] [5, 9, 10, 82] |
| merge pairs | [3, 5, 9, 10, 27, 38, 43, 82] |

Splitting needs no comparisons at all: all the sorting happens in the merges, and every merge gets two lists that are already sorted.

### The code
Using `merge` from above:
```python
def merge_sort(items):
    if len(items) <= 1:
        return items
    mid = len(items) // 2
    left = merge_sort(items[:mid])
    right = merge_sort(items[mid:])
    return merge(left, right)

print(merge_sort([38, 27, 43, 3, 9, 82, 10, 5]), merge_sort([]), merge_sort([2, 1]))
```
```output
[3, 5, 9, 10, 27, 38, 43, 82] [] [1, 2]
```
`mid = len(items) // 2` splits a list of 5 into 2 and 3 items. The base case, `len(items) <= 1`, is what stops the recursion: without it, a list of one item would split into an empty list and itself, forever. The slices `items[:mid]` and `items[mid:]` are new lists, so merge sort returns a **new** sorted list and leaves the original as it was. Animation 2 follows the calls.

### Why O(n log n)
Each split halves the lists, so after about log₂ n rounds of splitting every list has one item: a list of 8 items takes 3 levels, and a million items take 20. On each level, the merges handle every item once, which is O(n) work per level. That gives O(n log n) in all:

| n | levels, about log₂ n | about n × log₂ n | n × n, for an O(n²) sort |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 8 | 3 | 24 | 64 |
| 1,000 | 10 | 10,000 | 1,000,000 |
| 1,000,000 | 20 | 20,000,000 | 1,000,000,000,000 |

Merge sort always splits all the way down and merges everything back up, whatever the order of the items, so it's O(n log n) in the best, average and worst case.

### The cost: extra space
Bubble, selection and insertion sort work inside the list they're given, with O(1) extra space. Merge sort can't merge that way: `merge` builds a new list, and the slices copy the halves. At any moment it holds O(n) extra items, plus O(log n) calls waiting on the call stack. That's the price for the speed: O(n log n) time for O(n) extra space. When memory is tight, quicksort, the next lesson, sorts in place instead.

### A stable sort
On a tie, `merge` takes the item from the left list first. Every item in the left half came before every item in the right half in the original list, so equal items never pass each other: merge sort is **stable**. Here records are merged by age:
```python
def merge_by_age(left, right):
    merged = []
    i = 0
    j = 0
    while i < len(left) and j < len(right):
        if left[i][1] <= right[j][1]:
            merged.append(left[i])
            i += 1
        else:
            merged.append(right[j])
            j += 1
    return merged + left[i:] + right[j:]

print(merge_by_age([("Ana", 25), ("Cy", 30)], [("Ben", 25), ("Dee", 30)]))
```
```output
[('Ana', 25), ('Ben', 25), ('Cy', 30), ('Dee', 30)]
```
Ana and Ben are both 25, and Ana, from the left list, comes first. With `<` instead of `<=`, a tie would take Ben first, and equal items would swap order.

### Merge sort in practice
Merge sort is fast on any input, and stable, so it's the base of many real sorting functions. Python's `sorted()` and `.sort()` use **Timsort**, a merge sort that first finds stretches of the list that are already in order, sorts short stretches with insertion sort, and merges them. That makes it O(n log n) in the worst case and O(n) on a list that's already sorted.

Merging also works well when the data doesn't fit in memory: sort chunks that do fit, write them out, then merge the sorted chunks. And it suits linked lists, where merging relinks nodes without any copying.

=== keypoints
- Merging two sorted lists: compare the two front items, take the smaller (the left one on a tie), and when one list runs out, add the rest of the other. O(n).
- Merge sort: a list of 0 or 1 items is sorted. Otherwise split it at `mid = len(items) // 2`, merge sort each half, and merge the two sorted halves.
- About log₂ n levels of splitting, with O(n) merging work per level: O(n log n) in the best, average and worst case.
- O(n) extra space for the merged lists and slices: the price for the speed.
- Stable: on a tie, merge takes the left item first.

=== example: Watching the calls
intro: This merge sort prints each call's list as the call starts, and each merged result as the call returns, indented by how deep the call is in the recursion.
```python
def merge(left, right):
    merged = []
    i = 0
    j = 0
    while i < len(left) and j < len(right):
        if left[i] <= right[j]:
            merged.append(left[i])
            i += 1
        else:
            merged.append(right[j])
            j += 1
    return merged + left[i:] + right[j:]

def merge_sort(items, depth):
    print("  " * depth + "sort", items)
    if len(items) <= 1:
        return items
    mid = len(items) // 2
    left = merge_sort(items[:mid], depth + 1)
    right = merge_sort(items[mid:], depth + 1)
    result = merge(left, right)
    print("  " * depth + "merged", result)
    return result

merge_sort([3, 1, 4, 2], 0)
```
1-12: The usual merge.
14-15: `depth` counts how many calls are waiting above this one, and each level of depth indents the line by two spaces.
16-17: Lists of one item are already sorted, so those calls print their list and return at once.
19-20: The left half is sorted completely, with all its own calls, before the right half starts.
21-22: Each call merges its two sorted halves and prints the result.
25: The first call has depth 0, so its lines aren't indented.

=== example: Counting comparisons
intro: This version of merge sort also counts the comparisons it makes. It sorts lists in reverse order of three sizes, and prints each size, merge sort's comparisons, and the n × (n − 1) / 2 comparisons that insertion sort needs for the same list.
```python
def sort_count(items):
    if len(items) <= 1:
        return items, 0
    mid = len(items) // 2
    left, a = sort_count(items[:mid])
    right, b = sort_count(items[mid:])
    merged = []
    i = 0
    j = 0
    count = a + b
    while i < len(left) and j < len(right):
        count += 1
        if left[i] <= right[j]:
            merged.append(left[i])
            i += 1
        else:
            merged.append(right[j])
            j += 1
    return merged + left[i:] + right[j:], count

for n in [8, 64, 1024]:
    result, comparisons = sort_count(list(range(n, 0, -1)))
    print(n, comparisons, n * (n - 1) // 2)
```
1-2: `sort_count` returns two things: the sorted list and the number of comparisons.
5-6: Each half comes back with its own count, `a` and `b`.
10-12: The count starts with the halves' comparisons and goes up by one for each comparison in this merge.
21-23: For a list in reverse order, every left item is bigger than every right item, so each merge takes the whole right list first, one comparison per item.
23: For 1,024 items, merge sort makes 5,120 comparisons, where insertion sort makes over half a million.

